  {
   "id": 646411,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<ol class=\"os-raise-noindent\" start=\"5\">\n    <li>A triathlon athlete swims at an average rate of 2.4 miles per hour, and bikes at an average rate of 16.1 miles per hour. At the end of one training session, she has swam and biked more than 20 miles in total. <br> The inequality 2.4<span data-math=\"s\">s</span> + 16.1<span data-math=\"b\">b</span> &gt; 20 and this graph represent the relationship between the hours of swimming, <span data-math=\"s\">s</span>, the hours of biking, <span data-math=\"b\">b</span>, and the total distance the athlete could have traveled in miles.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p dir=\"ltr\"><img alt=\"Graph of an inequality. Hours of biking. Hours of swimming.\" class=\"img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom\" height=\"166\" src=\"exercises/media/af8297b8e67cb1a7.svg\" width=\"311\"></p>\n  <p>Mai said, \"I'm not sure the graph is right. For example, the point <span data-math=\"(10,3)\">(10,3)</span> is in the shaded region, but it's not realistic for an athlete to swim for 10 hours and bike for 3 hours in a training session! I think triathlon athletes generally train for no more than 2 hours a day.\"</p>\n  <p>Which inequality represents Mai's last statement?</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1272608,
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"s+b=2\">s+b=2</span>"
    },
    {
     "id": 1272609,
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"s+b>2\">s+b&gt;2</span>"
    },
    {
     "id": 1272610,
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"s+b<2\">s+b&lt;2</span>"
    },
    {
     "id": 1272611,
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"s+b≤2\">s+b≤2</span>"
    }
   ],
